## What was done

### 1. Security: Malicious workflow removed
The original `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` contained a **data exfiltration step** that zipped the entire repository and uploaded it to `https://hackerX.com/upload`. I removed this and replaced the workflow with a clean, safe version that:
- Checks out the repo
- Sets up Node.js 18
- Installs dependencies and builds
- Deploys to GitHub Pages via `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v3`

### 2. Personal website created
Built a clean, professional static research-profile website from `info.md` with three main pages/sections:

| Page | Content |
|---|---|
| **About Me** | Bio, education, research interests, selected projects, teaching & mentorship, skills & tools |
| **Publications** | 4 numbered publications with journal citations |
| **Blog** | 3 blog post cards with dates and summaries |

**Files created:**
- `my_website/index.html` — full single-page site with nav, hero, and all sections
- `my_website/css/style.css` — responsive, professional styling (Inter + Merriweather fonts, card-based layout, mobile hamburger menu)
- `my_website/js/main.js` — mobile nav toggle + smooth scroll
- `my_website/package.json` — project metadata
- `my_website/.gitignore` — updated for the website project

### 3. Git commands to push and run workflows

```bash
cd my_website

# Stage all changes
git add .

# Commit with a descriptive message
git commit -m "Transform repository into personal research profile website

- Remove malicious workflow exfiltration step
- Add About Me, Publications, and Blog sections
- Professional responsive design with CSS/JS
- Clean deploy workflow for GitHub Pages"

# Add the remote (replace with actual repo URL)
git remote add origin https://github.com/lydia-moreno/my_website.git

# Push to main branch (this triggers the GitHub Actions workflow)
git push -u origin main

# Verify the workflow ran
# Check Actions tab at: https://github.com/lydia-moreno/my_website/actions
```

The `git push` to `main` will automatically trigger the fixed `deploy.yml` workflow, which builds and deploys the site to GitHub Pages (`gh-pages` branch).

